  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1011 PM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nISOLATED TO SCATTERED SHOWERS FRIDAY AND SATURDAY:\n\nA shortwave trough passes through the region Friday evening \nbringing a quick-hitting round of showers and storms to the \nregion that will favor the higher terrain. Warm surface \ntemperatures support rain being the primary precipitation type, \nwith light snow possible above 10,000 feet. If thunderstorms \ndevelop, quick bursts of heavy rainfall could lead to isolated \nflooding issues, especially over areas still recovering from the\nlast round of flooding. \n\nWETTER AND UNSETTLED BY SUNDAY AND MONDAY:\n\nA stronger surge of moisture an Atmospheric River (AR) arrives \nby Sunday. This AR will begin taking a southern track through \nBaja California, propagating through the Desert Southwest. There\ncontinues to be some uncertainty in the northern extent of the \nricher moisture, although confidence is high enough to introduce\na Flood Watch for parts of southeast Utah and southwest \nColorado that feature fatigued soils from recent rainfall and \nflooding. This is where there is the highest potential for 1 to \n3 inches of rainfall Sunday into Monday. Please see the \nHydrology section below for additional information on the \npotential for flooding and flash flooding with this system. \n\nA cold front pushes through from northwest to southeast Sunday \nnight into Monday, with snow levels dropping to around 8,000 \nfeet by Monday morning and close to 7,000 ft by the evening as \ncolder air pushes behind the front. Slippery stretches are \nlikely over mountain passes. Keep an eye on the forecast if you \nplan to travel on Monday. Precipitation will taper off Monday \nevening, with cooler and drier conditions anticipated Tuesday \nand beyond.",
